#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:1
msgid "Re-exports"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:3
msgid ""
"Let's start by explaining what are re-exports. To do so, we will use an "
"example where we are writing a library (named `lib`) with some types "
"dispatched in sub-modules:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:15
msgid "Users can import them like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:22
msgid ""
"But what if you want the types to be available directly at the crate root or "
"if we don't want the modules to be visible for users? That's where re-"
"exports come in:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:26
msgid "// `sub_module1` and `sub_module2` are not visible outside.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:32
msgid "// We re-export both types:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:38
msgid "And now users will be able to do:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:44
msgid ""
"And since both `sub_module1` and `sub_module2` are private, users won't be "
"able to import them."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:46
msgid ""
"Now what's interesting is that the generated documentation for this crate "
"will show both `Foo` and `AnotherFoo` directly at the crate root, meaning "
"they have been inlined. There are a few rules to know whether or not a re-"
"exported item will be inlined."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:50
msgid "Inlining rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:52
msgid "If a public item comes from a private module, it will be inlined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:59
msgid "// `Public` will inlined here since `private_module` is private.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:61
msgid "// `Public` will not be inlined here since `public_mod` is public.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:66
msgid ""
"Likewise, if an item inherits `#[doc(hidden)]` from any of its ancestors, it "
"will be inlined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:72
msgid ""
"// `Public` be inlined since its parent (`public_mod`) has "
"`#[doc(hidden)]`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:77
msgid ""
"If an item has `#[doc(hidden)]`, it won't be inlined (nor visible in the "
"generated documentation):"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:80
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:92
msgid "// This struct won't be visible.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:83
msgid "// This re-export won't be visible.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:88
msgid ""
"However, if you still want the re-export itself to be visible, you can add "
"the `#[doc(inline)]` attribute on it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:100
msgid ""
"In this case, you will have `pub use self::Hidden as InlinedHidden;` in the "
"generated documentation but no link to the `Hidden` item."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:103
msgid ""
"So back to `#[doc(hidden)]`: if you have multiple re-exports and some of "
"them have `#[doc(hidden)]`, then these ones (and only these) won't appear in "
"the documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:108
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:160
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:193
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:207
msgid "/// First\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:111
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:163
msgid "/// Second\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:114
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:167
msgid "/// Third\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:119
msgid ""
"In this case, `InPrivate` will be inlined as `Visible`. However, its "
"documentation will be `First Third` and not `First Second Third` because the "
"re-export with `Second` as documentation has `#[doc(hidden)]`, therefore, "
"all its attributes are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:123
msgid "Inlining with `#[doc(inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:125
msgid ""
"You can use the `#[doc(inline)]` attribute if you want to force an item to "
"be inlined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:135
msgid ""
"With this code, even though `public_mod::Public` is public and present in "
"the documentation, the `Public` type will be present both at the crate root "
"and in the `public_mod` module."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:138
msgid "Preventing inlining with `#[doc(no_inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:140
msgid ""
"On the opposite of the `#[doc(inline)]` attribute, if you want to prevent an "
"item from being inlined, you can use `#[doc(no_inline)]`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:151
msgid ""
"In the generated documentation, you will see a re-export at the crate root "
"and not the type directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:154
msgid "Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:156
msgid ""
"When an item is inlined, its doc comments and most of its attributes will be "
"inlined along with it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:174
msgid ""
"In this case, `Visible` will have as documentation `First Second Third` and "
"will also have as `cfg`: `#[cfg(a, b, c)]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:177
msgid ""
"[Intra-doc links](./linking-to-items-by-name.md) are resolved relative to "
"where the doc comment is defined."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:180
msgid "There are a few attributes which are not inlined though:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:181
msgid "`#[doc(alias=\"\")]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:182
msgid "`#[doc(inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:183
msgid "`#[doc(no_inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:184
msgid ""
"`#[doc(hidden)]` (because the re-export itself and its attributes are "
"ignored)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:186
msgid ""
"All other attributes are inherited when inlined, so that the documentation "
"matches the behavior if the inlined item was directly defined at the spot "
"where it's shown."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:189
msgid "These rules also apply if the item is inlined with a glob re-export:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:202
msgid ""
"Otherwise, the attributes displayed will be from the re-exported item and "
"the attributes on the re-export itself will be ignored:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:216
msgid "In the above case, `cfg(c)` will not be displayed in the docs."
msgstr ""
